= Presupposition — Simple =

A '''presupposition''' is information that a speaker treats as already in the background.

If someone says “Maya stopped smoking,” the main message is that she stopped. The sentence also normally assumes that Maya used to smoke.

Presuppositions often survive changes such as negation. “Maya did not stop smoking” can still suggest that she had been smoking.

Sometimes listeners accept a new background assumption so the conversation makes sense. This is called accommodation.

Presupposition is not the same as [[Implicature/Simple|implicature]] or simple logical entailment.

